@* Why is this package here (TFND-7 / F6.2).

   The single most-asked question about an SBOM, and the one a flat dependency
   list cannot answer. A team told they have a critical CVE in a transitive
   package they have never heard of needs to know what pulled it in before they
   can do anything about it at all.

   Read downwards: the leftmost node is something the team chose to depend on,
   and the rightmost is the package in question. The first node is the one they
   can actually act on. *@

<div class="card-body">
    <h3>Why is this here?</h3>

    @if (!Paths.HasGraph)
    {
        @* Not the same as "nothing depends on this", and the difference
           matters: this is a limitation of whatever produced the SBOM, not a
           claim about the build. *@
        <p class="field__help">
            This SBOM lists components but carries no dependency edges, so the
            path cannot be reconstructed. Some producers emit a flat component
            list — <code class="mono">syft</code> with a directory scan, for
            instance. A producer that walks the lock file emits the graph.
        </p>
    }
    else if (Paths.Direct)
    {
        <p class="field__help">
            A <strong>direct</strong> dependency — nothing else pulled it in.
            Whoever added it to the manifest is who can remove it.
        </p>
    }
    else if (Paths.Paths.Count == 0)
    {
        <p class="field__help">Not present in this project's most recent SBOM.</p>
    }
    else
    {
        <ol class="dep-paths">
            @foreach (var path in Paths.Paths)
            {
                <li>
                    <span class="dep-path">
                        @for (var i = 0; i < path.Count; i++)
                        {
                            var node = path[i];
                            var last = i == path.Count - 1;

                            @* The FIRST node is emphasised, not the last. The
                               last one is what the reader already clicked on;
                               the first is the direct dependency they can
                               actually change. *@
                            <span class="mono @(i == 0 ? "dep-path__root" : null)"
                                  style="@(last ? "color: var(--color-high)" : null)">
                                @node.Name<span class="muted">@@@node.Version</span>
                            </span>
                            @if (!last)
                            {
                                <span class="muted dep-path__arrow">&rarr;</span>
                            }
                        }
                    </span>
                </li>
            }
        </ol>

        <p class="field__help">
            @if (Paths.Truncated)
            {
                <text>
                    More routes reach this package than are shown. These are the
                    shortest — the ones with the fewest maintainers between you
                    and a fix.
                </text>
            }
            else
            {
                <text>
                    Shortest route first. The leftmost package is the one you
                    depend on directly, and the one to change.
                </text>
            }
        </p>
    }
</div>

@code {
    [Parameter, EditorRequired] public DependencyPaths Paths { get; set; } = DependencyPaths.None;
}
